            "list_items": "Regulating to zero PPM — held up as an ideal, even though it is an impossible feat for any food.\nReporting heavy-metal levels without comparing them to actual safety thresholds, making safe products look risky.\nFailing to distinguish total metal content from bioavailability — not all metals present in food are absorbed.\nSwitching measurement units (PPM vs. PPB) so direct comparison gets confusing. PPM is the standard.\nCA Prop 65 uses a \"Threshold for Safety\" that over-regulates by a factor of 1000 — the strictest method there is.",
            "outro": "",
            "image_placeholder": ""
          }
